blem is \"condensed tree stability\" - it's a known algorithm from HDBSCAN. The condensed tree is built by selecting clusters with size >= min_cluster_size. Then for each cluster, stability = sum_{p in cluster} (lambda_{p,death} - lambda_{cluster,birth}) where lambda_{p,death} is the lambda at which point p leaves the cluster (i.e., either becomes noise or moves to a descendant cluster). In HDBSCAN's condensed tree, each point in a cluster has a value lambda_max (the lambda at which it last belonged to that cluster) which is either the lambda at which it leaves (if it goes to a child or becomes noise) or infinity if it remains in the cluster until the end (the root). For leaf clusters, points exit only when they become noise. For internal clusters, points may exit to children.\n\nIn the given payload, each cluster has `point_exit_lambdas`. This likely lists the lambda at which each point in that cluster *leaves* that cluster (i.e., either by moving to a child or by becoming noise). The size of the cluster is the total number of points belonging to that cluster at its birth. The number of point_exit_lambdas may be less than size because some points never leave (they persist to the root? But root has no parent). Typically, for a cluster that is not the root, all points eventually leave (either to a child or to noise). For the root, some points may never leave (they persist as noise? but root is the top). Actually, in HDBSCAN, the root contains all points, and points that never become noise or never leave to any child remain in the root forever (infinite lambda). So the root would have no point_exit_lambdas? But in the example, root c0 has point_exit_lambdas. So maybe these are the lambdas at which points *become noise* within that cluster? Or they are the lambdas at which points *descend* to a child? That doesn't align.\n\nAnother possibility: The `point_exit_lambdas` are actually the `lambda_max` values for the points in that cluster, i.e., the lambda at which each p