mber of points that belong to it. So it's safe to require that the lists be of length size.\n\nThus, the algorithm:\n\n1. Extract `min_cluster_size`, `clusters`, and `parent` from the payload.\n2. Validate: all required fields present, types correct, etc.\n3. Filter clusters where size >= min_cluster_size. Retain those.\n4. For each retained cluster, compute stability:\n   - Ensure cluster has `birth_lambda` and `point_exit_lambdas` list of length == size.\n   - For each exit_lambda in point_exit_lambdas, compute contribution = max(0, exit_lambda - birth_lambda).\n   - Sum contributions -> stability.\n5. Create a list of objects with cluster_id, size, stability.\n6. Rank clusters by decreasing stability, and in case of tie, by cluster_id (lexicographic? string). The \"cluster ID\" is a string, so tie-break by ascending cluster_id.\n7. Then select clusters using a greedy approach:\n   - Sort candidate clusters (retained ones) by descending stability, then ascending cluster_id.\n   - Maintain a set `selected = []` and a set `blocked_ancestors` (or we can mark ancestors as blocked). Actually the rule: \"Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it, then block its ancestors.\" So we need a way to check descendants and ancestors.\n   - For each cluster in sorted order:\n        - Check if any selected cluster is a descendant of this cluster? Wait \"no already selected descendant blocks it\" means if any selected cluster is a descendant of current cluster, then current cluster cannot be selected because descendant blocks it? The phrase: \"Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it, then block its ancestors.\" That's ambiguous. Usually in HDBSCAN, you iterate through clusters in decreasing stability, and you add a cluster if none of its descendants have been selected; when you add it, you mark all its ancestors as ineligible (i.e., you cannot select an ancestor). So \"blocks\" means if a descendant is already selected, the ancestor is blocked. So the check is: if any selected cluster is an ancestor of current? Or descendant? Let's parse: \"Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it\". That means: current cluster is only selectable if there is no already selected cluster that is a descendant of it. Because if a descendant is already selected, that descendant blocks the current cluster? But the usual HDBSCAN selection: you cannot have both a parent and child selected; you select the child if it is more stable, and then you block the parent. So if you are considering a parent, and a child is already selected, the parent is blocked. So the parent cannot be selected if a descendant is already selected. So the condition is: if any selected cluster is a descendant of the current cluster, then the current cluster is blocked. That matches \"no already selected descendant blocks it\". So we check if any selected cluster is a descendant of current. If not, we select current, and then we block its ancestors: meaning we mark all ancestors of current as ineligible for future selection (but since we process in order, we can just remove ancestors from the list or skip them if we encounter them later). To implement, we can maintain a set of blocked cluster IDs. After selecting a cluster, we traverse up the parent chain (using parent mapping) and add all ancestors to blocked set. Then when considering a new cluster, if it is in blocked set (i.e., it is an ancestor of a selected cluster), skip it. Also, we need to check if it has a descendant already selected? We'll do both: we can precompute descendant relationships? Actually the \"descendant\" check: for a given cluster, we need to know if any selected cluster is its descendant. If we maintain a set of selected cluster IDs, we could check if any selected ID is a descendant of current. That requires descendant relationship. We can build a tree structure once: for each cluster ID, store its parent and children. Then we can traverse. Or simpler: when we select a cluster, we block its ancestors. So any cluster that is an ancestor of a selected cluster gets blocked. Therefore, when we encounter a cluster later, we just check if it is blocked. That will prevent selecting any ancestor. But what about selecting a descendant of an already selected cluster? If we have selected a cluster, and later consider a descendant (which is more specific), that descendant is not an ancestor of the selected cluster, so it's not blocked. However, the condition says \"no already selected descendant blocks it\" – that's about the current cluster being blocked by a descendant. If we have selected a cluster, and later we consider a descendant of that selected cluster, then the selected cluster is an ancestor of the descendant, not a descendant. So the descendant would not be blocked by its ancestor; rather the ancestor would have been blocked if descendant was selected first. Since we are selecting in order of stability, if a descendant has higher stability, it would be selected first, blocking the ancestor. If a descendant has lower stability, it will be considered after the ancestor. At that point, the ancestor is already selected, and the descendant is not an ancestor of the selected cluster, so it would not be blocked. But we need to ensure that a descendant cannot be selected if its ancestor is already selected. That is the reverse: \"no already selected ancestor blocks it\". The rule as written: \"Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it\" – that seems one-sided. But typical HDBSCAN selection picks the cluster if it doesn't have any selected child (descendant). So the condition is exactly that: if any selected cluster is a descendant, the current cluster is blocked. It does not block a descendant from being selected if its ancestor is already selected. However, normally we select in order of stability, so if a child has higher stability, it gets selected first and blocks the parent. I